CAT = "sqeCrimPractice"; SEG = "CRP"
Q = [
("A suspect is arrested and taken to the police station. From when do his detention limits run?",
 "Arrival at the station", ["Time of the arrest", "Start of the interview", "His first legal advice"],
 "Under s.41 PACE the relevant time is usually arrival at the first police station after arrest, and the basic limit is 24 hours without charge."),
("A suspect is held for an indictable offence. To how long may a superintendent extend detention without charge?",
 "36 hours", ["48 hours", "72 hours", "96 hours"],
 "Under s.42 PACE a superintendent may authorise detention up to 36 hours for an indictable offence; longer needs a warrant from magistrates, to a maximum of 96 hours."),
("A suspect stays silent in interview, then relies at trial on a fact he could have mentioned. What may the court do?",
 "Draw an adverse inference", ["Exclude the defence entirely", "Treat it as a confession", "Order a retrial"],
 "Section 34 CJPOA 1994 allows the court or jury to draw such inferences as appear proper, after a proper caution and access to legal advice."),
("Who cannot act as appropriate adult for a 16-year-old suspect at interview?",
 "His solicitor", ["His mother", "A social worker", "A trained volunteer"],
 "Code C says a solicitor acting for the suspect in a professional capacity may not be the appropriate adult, since the roles differ."),
("An adult charged with burglary asks about bail. What is the starting point?",
 "A right to bail", ["Remand in custody", "Bail only with a surety", "Bail only if he pleads guilty"],
 "Section 4 Bail Act 1976 gives a general right to bail, subject to the exceptions in Schedule 1, such as substantial grounds to believe he would fail to surrender."),
("An adult pleads not guilty to an either-way offence and magistrates accept jurisdiction. What happens next?",
 "He chooses his trial venue", ["He is tried by magistrates", "He is committed for sentence", "The case is discontinued"],
 "Once the magistrates decide summary trial is suitable, the defendant chooses whether to accept it or elect trial on indictment."),
("An adult first appears at magistrates' court charged with robbery. What happens?",
 "Sent to the Crown Court", ["Tried by the magistrates", "Asked to choose a venue", "Bailed to youth court"],
 "Robbery is indictable only, so under s.51 Crime and Disorder Act 1998 the case is sent straight to the Crown Court."),
("The defence attacks a prosecution witness's character. Which gateway may admit the defendant's bad character?",
 "Attack on another's character", ["Explanatory evidence", "Correcting a false impression", "Agreed by all parties"],
 "Under s.101(1)(g) Criminal Justice Act 2003 the defendant's bad character becomes admissible where he has made an attack on another person's character."),
("A confession was obtained by oppression. What must the court do with it?",
 "Exclude it entirely", ["Admit it with a warning", "Admit it if likely true", "Leave it to the jury"],
 "Section 76(2) PACE requires exclusion where a confession may have been obtained by oppression, unless the prosecution proves beyond reasonable doubt that it was not."),
("The case rests on a disputed eyewitness identification. What must the judge give the jury?",
 "A Turnbull warning", ["A Lucas direction", "A Newton hearing", "A majority direction"],
 "R v Turnbull requires a warning about the special need for caution before convicting on disputed identification evidence."),
("A defendant pleads guilty at the first stage of proceedings. What is the most sentence reduction he can get?",
 "One third", ["One quarter", "One half", "One tenth"],
 "The Sentencing Council guideline gives a maximum one third reduction for a plea at the first stage, falling to one quarter later and less at trial."),
("A defendant convicted in the magistrates' court after pleading not guilty wants to appeal the verdict. Where does it go?",
 "Crown Court rehearing", ["Court of Appeal", "Supreme Court", "Divisional Court only"],
 "A defendant may appeal against conviction as of right to the Crown Court, which rehears the case. Appeals on law alone can go by case stated."),
]
